    stem: 'A 68-year-old right-handed woman is brought to the emergency department by her daughter due to sudden-onset left-sided weakness and severe slurred speech. The daughter states that the patient went to sleep in her usual state of health at 10:00 PM last night and awoke this morning at 6:00 AM with dense left hemiplegia and facial droop ("wake-up stroke"). On arrival at 7:30 AM, vital signs are: blood pressure 162/92 mmHg, heart rate 82/min, and oxygen saturation 98% on ambient air. Neurological examination reveals: severe dysarthria, left hemineglect, left facial droop, and flaccid left hemiparalysis (muscle strength 0/5 in left arm and leg), with an NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS) score of 17. Fingerstick blood glucose is 124 mg/dL. Non-contrast brain CT demonstrates an Alberta Stroke Program Early CT Score (ASPECTS) of 8 without intracranial hemorrhage. Immediate CT angiography (CTA) of the head and neck reveals a complete occlusion of the right proximal middle cerebral artery (MCA, M1 segment). CT perfusion imaging demonstrates an ischemic core volume of 18 mL and a large hypoperfused penumbra volume of 92 mL (mismatch volume: 74 mL; mismatch ratio: 5.1). Which of the following is the most appropriate management plan for this patient?',
