    stem: 'A 56-year-old man presents to the primary care clinic for a routine health maintenance visit. He has no cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, fever, night sweats, or unintentional weight loss. His past medical history is notable for mild hypertension managed with hydrochlorothiazide. He smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 24 years (24 pack-year smoking history) and successfully quit smoking 4 years ago at age 52. Physical examination reveals normal vital signs, clear breath sounds bilaterally, and no lymphadenopathy or digital clubbing. The patient asks whether he qualifies for lung cancer screening. According to the current United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation for this patient?',
    choices: [
      ['Recommend annual screening for lung cancer with low-dose computed tomography (LDCT) of the chest', 'The 2021 USPSTF lung cancer screening guidelines recommend annual screening for lung cancer with low-dose computed tomography (LDCT) in adults who meet ALL three of the following criteria: (1) Age 50 to 80 years, (2) A smoking history of at least 20 pack-years, and (3) Currently smoke OR have quit smoking within the past 15 years. This patient is 56 years old (within the 50–80 age range), has a 24 pack-year smoking history (exceeding the 20 pack-year threshold), and quit smoking 4 years ago (well within the 15-year window). Therefore, he meets all criteria for annual LDCT screening. Screening is discontinued once a person reaches age 81, or has not smoked for 15 consecutive years, or develops a health problem that substantially limits life expectancy or the ability to undergo curative lung surgery.'],
      ['Reassure the patient that he does not qualify for screening because he has already quit smoking for more than 3 years', 'Screening is indicated for former smokers who quit within the past 15 years; having quit 4 years ago does not disqualify him.'],
      ['Order a baseline posterior-anterior and lateral chest radiograph today, repeating every 2 years', 'Chest radiography (with or without sputum cytology) has zero proven mortality benefit for lung cancer screening and is explicitly NOT recommended by the USPSTF.'],
      ['Delay lung cancer screening until age 65, as the majority of lung malignancies occur in the seventh decade', 'The 2021 USPSTF update lowered the starting age from 55 to 50 years to expand early detection; delaying until age 65 misses curable early-stage resectable cancers.'],
      ['Order a serum carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) and circulating tumor DNA assay before considering imaging', 'Serum biomarkers and circulating tumor DNA are unapproved and under-sensitive for population lung cancer screening; LDCT is the sole proven modality.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'The USPSTF recommends annual low-dose CT (LDCT) screening for lung cancer in adults aged 50-80 years who have a >= 20 pack-year smoking history and currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years. This 56-year-old with a 24 pack-year history who quit 4 years ago meets all criteria. Chest X-rays have no mortality benefit and are not recommended.',
    objective: 'Apply the 2021 USPSTF lung cancer screening criteria: annual low-dose CT for individuals aged 50–80 with >= 20 pack-years who currently smoke or quit within 15 years.',
    takeaways: [
      'USPSTF criteria: age 50–80, >= 20 pack-years, current smoker or quit within 15 years.',
      'Modality: annual low-dose chest CT (LDCT).',
      'Chest X-rays do not reduce mortality and are not recommended.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'JAMA 2021 USPSTF Recommendation Statement: Screening for Lung Cancer', url: 'https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2777241' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Age 56 + 24 pack-years + quit 4 years ago -> meets USPSTF criteria -> annual low-dose CT (LDCT).',
    necessaryInference: 'Verify that the patient satisfies all 3 USPSTF criteria (age 50-80, >= 20 pack-years, quit < 15 years).',
    strongestDistractor: 'Chest radiography, an obsolete screening modality that fails to reduce mortality.',
    distractorExclusion: 'Chest X-ray does not reduce lung cancer mortality; low-dose CT is the only evidence-based screening imaging.',
