    choices: [
      ['Intravenous andexanet alfa (coagulation factor Xa [recombinant], inactivated-zhzo)', 'Apixaban and rivaroxaban are direct oral factor Xa inhibitors. In patients experiencing life-threatening, uncontrolled bleeding (such as intracranial hemorrhage) or requiring emergent surgery, rapid and specific reversal of factor Xa inhibition is required. Andexanet alfa is a recombinant, modified, catalytically inactive human factor Xa decoy protein that binds and sequesters direct factor Xa inhibitors (apixaban and rivaroxaban) with high affinity, rapidly neutralizing their anticoagulant effect and restoring endogenous thrombin generation. If andexanet alfa is unavailable, four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC, unactivated) is the recommended alternative. Idarucizumab is the specific reversal agent for dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor) and has NO activity against factor Xa inhibitors.'],
      ['Intravenous idarucizumab (humanized monoclonal antibody Fab fragment)', 'Idarucizumab specifically binds and neutralizes dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor); it does NOT bind or reverse direct factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban or rivaroxaban.'],
      ['Intravenous protamine sulfate infusion', 'Protamine sulfate reverses unfractionated heparin and partially reverses low-molecular-weight heparin; it has zero efficacy against oral direct factor Xa inhibitors.'],
      ['Intravenous phytomenadione (vitamin K1) and fresh frozen plasma', 'Vitamin K requires 12–24 hours to stimulate hepatic synthesis of clotting factors and reverses only vitamin K antagonists (warfarin); it does not reverse direct factor Xa inhibitors.'],
      ['Intravenous desmopressin (DDAVP) and aminocaproic acid', 'DDAVP stimulates endothelial release of vWF/factor VIII and is used for uremic bleeding, von Willebrand disease, or antiplatelet reversal; it does not reverse factor Xa inhibition.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Apixaban is a direct factor Xa inhibitor. Life-threatening intracranial bleeding requires immediate reversal. Andexanet alfa is a recombinant decoy factor Xa protein that specifically binds and reverses apixaban and rivaroxaban. If unavailable, 4-factor PCC is used. Idarucizumab specifically reverses dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor), not factor Xa inhibitors. Protamine reverses heparin; vitamin K reverses warfarin.',
    objective: 'Select andexanet alfa as the specific reversal agent for direct factor Xa inhibitors (apixaban, rivaroxaban) in life-threatening bleeding.',
    takeaways: [
      'Factor Xa inhibitor (apixaban, rivaroxaban) reversal: andexanet alfa (or 4-factor PCC).',
      'Direct thrombin inhibitor (dabigatran) reversal: idarucizumab.',
      'Warfarin reversal: 4-factor PCC + intravenous vitamin K.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Circulation 2020 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway on Management of Bleeding in Patients on Oral Anticoagulants', url: 'https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2020.04.053' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Life-threatening bleed on apixaban (Factor Xa inhibitor) -> administer andexanet alfa (specific decoy reversal).',
    necessaryInference: 'Differentiate factor Xa inhibitor reversal (andexanet alfa) from direct thrombin inhibitor reversal (idarucizumab).',
    strongestDistractor: 'Idarucizumab, the specific reversal agent for dabigatran, which has zero activity against apixaban.',
